{SECT 0 {EXCHG {PARA 212 "" 0 "" {TEXT 411 31 "Lecture 4 Basic Data S
tructures" }}}{SECT 1 {PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT 412 27 "The concatenation \+
operator " }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 26 "Another difference i
s the " }{TEXT 214 22 "concatenation operator" }{TEXT 215 2 ". " }
{TEXT 413 18 " In version since " }{TEXT 216 7 "Maple 6" }{TEXT 413 3 
",  " }{TEXT 217 6 "x || y" }{TEXT 413 36 " gives the concatenation of
 strings " }{TEXT 218 1 "x" }{TEXT 413 5 " and " }{TEXT 219 1 "y" }
{TEXT 413 48 ". In previous versions of Maple it was given by " }{TEXT
 220 3 "x.y" }{TEXT 413 92 ".  Concatenation is useful for forming a l
arge number of variables. Here's a simple example:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 
"> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 21 "for i from 1 to 5 do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 " \+
 x||i:=i^2;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "end do;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 413 79 "We have created the five variables x1,x2,x3,x4,x5 and as
signed to xi the value " }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting
:-msup(Typesetting:-mi(\"i\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \"itali
c\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"2\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), superscriptshi
ft = \"0\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6
#-I%msupGF$6%-I#miGF$6%Q\"iF'/%'italicGQ%trueF'/%,mathvariantGQ'italic
F'-I#mnGF$6$Q\"2F'/F6Q'normalF'/%1superscriptshiftGQ\"0F'" }{TEXT 413 
1 "." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 
0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "x1,x2,x3,x4,x5;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT
 413 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 221 79 "The Maple help page for || s
tates that the use of this operator is discouraged." }{TEXT 413 124 " \+
 In Lecture 3 we discussed how to use indexed variables. In the next s
ection we say a little more about indexed variables. " }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 8 "
Usually " }{TEXT 222 9 "cat(x,y) " }{TEXT 413 14 "is the same as" }
{TEXT 223 7 " x || y" }{TEXT 413 16 ". The  function " }{TEXT 224 3 "c
at" }{TEXT 413 124 " may also be useful to get rid of extra commas whe
n printing combinations of text and integers, as in the following exam
ple:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "restart;" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 21 "for i from 1 to 3 do\n" }{MPLTEXT 
1 0 44 "  print(`If i = `, i, ` then i^2 = `, i^2);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 
54 "  print(cat(`If i = `, i, ` then i^2 = `, i^2, `.`));\n" }{MPLTEXT
 1 0 55 "  print(cat(`If i = `, i, `, then i^2 = `, i^2, `.`));\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 57 "  print(`--------------------------------------------
`);\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "end do;" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 13 "But actually " }{TEXT 225 6 "printf" }
{TEXT 413 56 " which we will discuss later is better for such matters.
" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
413 14 "Note that the " }{TEXT 226 3 "cat" }{TEXT 413 72 " operator wi
ll not work nicely with floating point numbers. For example:" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "x||1.0:=3;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "cat(x,1.0):=3;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 ""
 {TEXT 413 20 "But this works fine:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "x||1:=3; " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 24 "But
 note that if we use " }{TEXT 227 3 "cat" }{TEXT 413 25 " we get an er
ror message:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "restart:\n" 
}{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "cat(x,1):=3; " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 
10 "Check out " }{TEXT 228 4 "?cat" }{TEXT 413 19 " for more examples.
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}}{SECT 1 {PARA 3 ""
 0 "" {TEXT 412 17 "Indexed variables" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "
" }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 57 "An alternative way to create \+
several variables is to use " }{TEXT 229 17 "indexed variables" }{TEXT
 413 34 ". These are variables of the form " }{TEXT 230 4 "x[i]" }
{TEXT 413 25 " which Maple exhibits as " }{XPPEDIT 209 0 "Typesetting:
-mrow(Typesetting:-msub(Typesetting:-mi(\"x\", italic = \"true\", font
_style_name = \"CharacterStyle259\", mathvariant = \"italic\"), Typese
tting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"i\", italic = \"true\", font_style_name \+
= \"CharacterStyle259\", mathvariant = \"italic\")), subscriptshift = \+
\"0\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+modulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6#-I%m
subGF$6%-I#miGF$6&Q\"xF'/%'italicGQ%trueF'/%0font_style_nameGQ2Charact
erStyle259F'/%,mathvariantGQ'italicF'-F#6#-F/6&Q\"iF'F2F5F8/%/subscrip
tshiftGQ\"0F'" }{TEXT 231 2 " ." }{TEXT 413 15 "  That is, the " }
{TEXT 232 1 "i" }{TEXT 413 156 " becomes a subscript.  This is the sam
e in Maple 6, 7, 8, and 9 as in previous versions of Maple.  [although
 the notation is similar to that for arrays, an " }{TEXT 233 16 "index
ed variable" }{TEXT 413 11 " is not an " }{TEXT 234 5 "array" }{TEXT 
413 80 ". We will discuss the distinction later. Indexed variables are
 actually of type " }{TEXT 235 5 "table" }{TEXT 413 2 ".]" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "restart;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 21 "for i from 1 to 5 do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "  x[i]:=i
^2;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "end do;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 
89 "One may also use doubly, triply, etc., subscribed variables as in \+
the following examples:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "
A[2,5]:=2;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "z[1,2,2,3]:=x
^2 + sin(x);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}}{SECT 
1 {PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT 412 10 "Sequences " }{TEXT 236 25 "(max, min, \+
seq, ithprime)" }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0
 "" {TEXT 237 27 "Perhaps the most important " }{TEXT 238 5 "types" }
{TEXT 239 4 " or " }{TEXT 240 7 "classes" }{TEXT 241 25 " of objects i
n Maple are " }{TEXT 242 16 "sequences, sets " }{TEXT 243 4 "and " }
{TEXT 244 5 "lists" }{TEXT 245 44 ". It is vital to understand these t
horoughly" }{TEXT 413 2 ". " }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 31 "We start with some examples of " 
}{TEXT 209 9 "sequences" }{TEXT 413 4 ": A " }{TEXT 246 8 "sequence" }
{TEXT 413 3 "  (" }{TEXT 247 3 "aka" }{TEXT 413 4 " an " }{TEXT 248 
10 "expression" }{TEXT 413 1 " " }{TEXT 249 8 "sequence" }{TEXT 413 
151 ") is just a bunch of expressions separated by commas: The express
ions may be numbers, variables, functions, etc. -- and they need not a
ll be different." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "restart;
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 30 "sequence1:=1,2,1,A,3,x,
sin(t);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 3 "If " }{TEXT 250 9 "seq
uence1" }{TEXT 413 20 " is a sequence then " }{TEXT 251 12 "sequence1[
i]" }{TEXT 413 47 " is the i-th term of the sequence, for example:" }}
}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 54 "sequence1[1], sequence1[2],
 sequence1[3],sequence1[7];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 26 "W
e  make another sequence:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 
24 "sequence2:=55,7,33,-1,4;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 90 "
The maximum and minimum of a sequence of real numbers is obtained by u
se of the functions " }{TEXT 252 3 "max" }{TEXT 413 5 " and " }{TEXT 
253 3 "min" }{TEXT 413 31 ", as in the following examples:" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 31 "min(sequence2), max(sequence2);" }}
}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT
 413 28 "Note that the solution to a " }{TEXT 254 5 "solve" }{TEXT 
413 51 " command as in the following example is given as a " }{TEXT 
255 8 "sequence" }{TEXT 413 58 ". We name it so we can manipulate the \+
different solutions:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 30 "p:=
x^3-60*x^2 + 1100*x - 6000;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 
24 "sequence3:=solve(p=0,x);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 191 
"Note that if the variable x is replaced by \{x\} then we get a differ
ent output but still a sequence. In this case a sequence of sets of th
e form \{x = a\} where a is a solution to the equation. " }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "solve(p=0,\{x\});" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 124 "Note that we can get hold of individual solut
ions as follows.  This is useful to keep from recopying complicated so
lutions. " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "sequence3[1]; 
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "sequence3[2];" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "sequence3[3];" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 256 13 "The function " }{TEXT 257 3 "seq" }{TEXT
 258 187 " is a very important way to form sequences. It can be used t
o do many things that can be done with do loops and when that's possib
le should be done. Generally this is a faster way to go. " }{TEXT 413 
96 "For example, if we wish to apply some function f to the numbers -1
, 2, 3, . . . , 10 we can use " }{TEXT 259 3 "seq" }{TEXT 413 12 " as \+
follows:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 30 "sequence4:=seq(
f(i),i=-1..10);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 16 "For example, \+
if " }{XPPEDIT 18 0 "Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"\"), Typesett
ing:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"\"), Typesetting:-mrow(Typesetting:-mi(\"f
\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \"italic\"), Typesetting:-mo(\"&A
pplyFunction;\", mathvariant = \"normal\", fence = \"false\", separato
r = \"false\", stretchy = \"false\", symmetric = \"false\", largeop = \+
\"false\", movablelimits = \"false\", accent = \"false\", lspace = \"0
.0em\", rspace = \"0.0em\"), Typesetting:-mfenced(Typesetting:-mrow(Ty
pesetting:-mi(\"x\", italic = \"true\", mathvariant = \"italic\")), ma
thvariant = \"normal\")), Typesetting:-mo(\"=\", mathvariant = \"norma
l\", fence = \"false\", separator = \"false\", stretchy = \"false\", s
ymmetric = \"false\", largeop = \"false\", movablelimits = \"false\", \+
accent = \"false\", lspace = \"0.2777778em\", rspace = \"0.2777778em\"
), Typesetting:-msup(Typesetting:-mi(\"x\", italic = \"true\", mathvar
iant = \"italic\"), Typesetting:-mn(\"2\", mathvariant = \"normal\"), \+
superscriptshift = \"0\")), Typesetting:-mi(\"\"));" "-I%mrowG6#/I+mod
ulenameG6\"I,TypesettingGI(_syslibGF'6%-I#miGF$6#Q!F'-F#6&F+-F#6%-F,6%
Q\"fF'/%'italicGQ%trueF'/%,mathvariantGQ'italicF'-I#moGF$6-Q0&ApplyFun
ction;F'/F:Q'normalF'/%&fenceGQ&falseF'/%*separatorGFD/%)stretchyGFD/%
*symmetricGFD/%(largeopGFD/%.movablelimitsGFD/%'accentGFD/%'lspaceGQ&0
.0emF'/%'rspaceGFS-I(mfencedGF$6$-F#6#-F,6%Q\"xF'F6F9F@-F=6-Q\"=F'F@FB
FEFGFIFKFMFO/FRQ,0.2777778emF'/FUF\\o-I%msupGF$6%Fen-I#mnGF$6$Q\"2F'F@
/%1superscriptshiftGQ\"0F'F+" }{TEXT 413 9 " we have:" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 29 "sequence4:=seq(i^2,i=-1..10);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 147 "We could also form this sequence
 with the following do loop.  We use s to indicate the sequence and we
 start by setting s to be the NULL sequence. " }{TEXT 260 40 "You shou
ld study this example carefully!" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT
 1 0 9 "s:=NULL;\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 23 "for i
 from -1 to 10 do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "  s:=s,i^2:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 
"end do:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "sequenc4:=s;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 413 9 "In Maple " }{TEXT 261 11 "ithprime(n)" }{TEXT 413 27 " is
 the n-th prime number. " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 55 
"ithprime(1), ithprime(2), ithprime(3), ithprime(1000); " }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 68 "We can get a sequence consisting of the \+
first  20 primes as follows:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 36 "sequence5:=seq(ithprime(k),k
=1..20);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 8 "One can " }{TEXT 262 
11 "concatenate" }{TEXT 413 23 " sequences as follows:\n" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "sequence4;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "seq
uence5;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 31 "sequence6:=seque
nce4,sequence5;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 27 "A simpler exa
mple would be:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "seqA:=a,b
,c;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "seqB:=x,y,z,w;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 21 "seqC:=seq
A,seqB,seqA;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 1 "\n" }{TEXT 413 
36 "Now consider the following sequence:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 27 "sequence7:=a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "
" {TEXT 413 74 "One can select more than one element of a sequence at \+
a time. For example:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "seq
uence7[2..4];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 42 "To get the firs
t element on the right use:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 
14 "sequence7[-1];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 45 "To get the
 second element from the right use:" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" 
}}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "sequence7[-2];" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 38 "We can use this to reverse a sequ
ence:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "S1:=seq(i,i=1..7);
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 23 "S2:=seq(S1[-i],i=1..7);
" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 31 "The empty sequence is given \+
by " }{TEXT 263 8 "NULL -- " }{TEXT 209 24 "as we already say above." 
}}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "S3:=NULL;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 
0 1 "\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "S4:=x,x,S3,y;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" 
{TEXT 413 12 "In general, " }{TEXT 264 17 "seq(f(i), i = X) " }{TEXT 
413 15 "is the same as " }{TEXT 265 28 "seq(f(X[i]), i=1..nops(X)). " 
}{TEXT 413 12 "For example:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 
14 "X:=[1,3,5,7]:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "seq(f(i), i = X);\n" }{MPLTEXT 
1 0 27 "seq(f(X[i]), i=1..nops(X));" }}}}{SECT 1 {PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT
 412 5 "Sets " }{TEXT 266 47 "(union, intersect, subset, nops, minus, \+
member)" }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 17 "One can obtain a " }
{TEXT 267 3 "set" }{TEXT 413 8 " from a " }{TEXT 268 8 "sequence" }
{TEXT 413 109 " by placing braces around it. We illustrate this below.
 We also illustrate some basic set operations such as " }{TEXT 269 48 
"union, intersect, minus, subset, nops and member" }{TEXT 413 2 ". " }
{TEXT 270 79 "Note, however, that the user has no control over the ord
er of elements of a set" }{TEXT 413 89 ". To control the order of elem
ents we must use sequences or lists --as we will see below:" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "restart:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "
> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "set1:=\{4,3,3,2,1\};" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> "
 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "set2:=\{4,4,5,5,6\};" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 
"" {MPLTEXT 1 0 22 "set3:=set1 union set2;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "set4:=set1 intersect set2;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 
0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 22 "set5:=set1 minus set2;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0
 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 17 "set5 subset set3;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 17 "set5 subset set4;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
413 60 "Let's form a sequence and then turn the sequence into a set." 
}}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "ss:=seq(i,i=0..9);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "set6:=\{ss\};" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 23 "Here's another example." }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "seq(x^j-1,j=1..5);" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "set7:=\{seq(x^j-1,j=1..5)\};" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 4 "The " }{TEXT 271 9 "empty set" }
{TEXT 413 19 " is denoted by \{\};\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "\{1,2,3\} intersect \{4,5,6\};" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT 272 4 "nops" }{TEXT 413 49 " is used to find the number of \+
elements in a set:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "nops(
set7);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "nops(\{\});" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 33 "Note that nops will not work for 
" }{TEXT 273 9 "sequences" }{TEXT 413 1 "!" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "nops(1,2,3,4);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
413 4 "But " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "nops(\{1,2,3
,4\});" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "t:=1,1,3,4,4;" }}
}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 78 "Note that the following does not
 give the number of elements in the sequence t" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> "
 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "nops(t);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 
86 "But we can convert to to a list and get the number of elements in \+
the list as follows:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "nop
s([t]);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 286 "If S is a set then S
[i] is the i-th element of the set, but you have no way of knowing wha
t order Maple assigns to the elements of the set S. And the order may \+
change in a single worksheet session. So do NOT make any assumptions a
bout the order even if you see the order on the monitor." }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 5 "set7;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 48 "for i from 1 to nops(set7) do i,set7[i]; end do;" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 274 10 "Membership" }{TEXT 413 31 " in a \+
set is tested as follows:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 5 
"set7;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "member(x^2-1,set7
);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 15 "member(x,set7);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 64 "Note carefully the following way \+
of putting x into the set set7:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 61 "if member(x,set7) = false then set7:=set7 union \{x\}; end if;
\n" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}{PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
413 85 "Recall the example in the previous section on the use of solve
. Let's repeat it here:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 30 "
p:=x^3-60*x^2 + 1100*x - 6000;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 24 "sequence3:=solve(p=0,x);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT
 1 0 14 "sequence3[1]; " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 112 "Now \+
we make a small change in the solve command. Namely we replace x  by \+
\{x\}: See the difference in the output. " }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 ""
 {MPLTEXT 1 0 26 "sequence4:=solve(p=0,\{x\});" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> "
 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 13 "sequence4[1];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
413 64 "This will be convenient for some purposes as we shall see late
r." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}}{SECT 1 {PARA 3 
"" 0 "" {TEXT 412 6 "Lists " }{TEXT 275 43 "(sort,  nops,  op,  concat
enation of lists)" }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 276 5 "Lists" }{TEXT
 277 1 " " }{TEXT 413 68 "may be formed by placing a sequence in squar
e brackets, as follows: " }{TEXT 278 36 "In this case order is all imp
ortant." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 23 "List1:=[4,0,2,2,
3,1,4];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }{TEXT 279 5 "sort "
 }{TEXT 413 58 " is useful for rearranging a list in non-decreasing or
der." }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 12 "sort(List1);" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 280 4 "nops" }{TEXT 413 43 "(List1) gives
 the length of the list List1:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 15 "N:=nops(List1);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 22 "Compa
re the following:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "nops([
1,3,3,3,3]);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 18 "nops(\{1,3,
3,3,3\});" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 20 "The i-th element in
 " }{TEXT 281 5 "List1" }{TEXT 413 13 " is given by " }{TEXT 282 9 "Li
st1[i]:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 ">
 " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 40 "for i from 1 to N do i,List1[i]; end do;" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 65 "We can select the list of element
 indexed from 2 to 4 as follows:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT
 1 0 12 "List1[2..4];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 104 "The ri
ght most element of the list has index -1. The second from the end has
 index -2, etc. For example:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 
0 7 "List1;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 31 "List1[-1],List1[-2], List1[-3];" }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 3 "To " }{TEXT 283 11 "concatenate" }
{TEXT 413 81 " lists we need to first recover the underlying sequence \+
by applying the function " }{TEXT 284 2 "op" }{TEXT 413 49 ". This als
o works for sets, as we now illustrate:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "List1; \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "op(List1);" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 6 "set1;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 9 "op(set1);"
 }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 7 "List1;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 
15 "List2:=[x,y,z];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 46 "Here's th
e way to concatenate List1 and List2:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 29 "List3:=[op(List1),op(List2)];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0
 "" {TEXT 413 53 "Here's how to add a new element to the end of a list
:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 35 "List4:=[op(List3),`a n
ew element`];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 4 "The " }{TEXT 
285 10 "empty list" }{TEXT 413 20 " is denoted by [ ] ;" }}{PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT 413 0 "" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 97 "Let's make
 a list of the primes numbers up to 100: We will put them in a list we
 will call List5:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 11 "List5:
=[]:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 23 "for i from 1 to 100 do\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 22 "
  if isprime(i) then \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 29 "      List5:=[op(List5),i];
 \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "  end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "end do;\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 6 "List5;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 52 "An eas
ier way to create the same list is as follows:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> "
 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "restart;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 
1 0 12 "seq5:=NULL:\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 23 "for i from 1 to 100 do\n" }
{MPLTEXT 1 0 22 "  if isprime(i) then \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 19 "    seq5:=
seq5,i; \n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "  end if;\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "end do;\n"
 }{MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "List5:=[seq5];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 
413 194 "As mentioned above one can also select elements of a list in \+
reverse order. For example, the last element of the list List is found
 by List[-1]. The second from last is given by List[-2], etc..." }}}
{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 25 "List:=[seq(i, i=1..10)];\n" 
}{MPLTEXT 1 0 29 "List[-1], List[-2], List[-3];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 
0 "" {TEXT 413 38 "We can obtain the reverse of the list " }{TEXT 286 
4 "List" }{TEXT 413 18 " above as follows:" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 33 "revList:=[seq(List[-i],i=1..10)];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 
0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 0 "" }}}}{SECT 1 {PARA 3 "" 0 "" {TEXT 412 
10 "Conversion" }}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 67 "One can convert
 lists to sets and vice versa by using the function " }{TEXT 287 7 "co
nvert" }{TEXT 413 1 ":" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 8 "re
start;" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 16 "L:=[1,4,1,2,3];\n
" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 22 "SetL:=convert(L, set);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "
" {MPLTEXT 1 0 14 "S:=\{1,2,3,5\};\n" }{MPLTEXT 1 0 23 "ListS:=convert
(S,list);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 20 "We can convert from
 " }{TEXT 288 3 "set" }{TEXT 413 5 " and " }{TEXT 289 4 "list" }{TEXT 
413 4 " to " }{TEXT 290 8 "sequence" }{TEXT 413 11 " by use of " }
{TEXT 291 2 "op" }{TEXT 413 27 " as already noted above or " }{TEXT 
292 5 "set[]" }{TEXT 413 5 " and " }{TEXT 293 6 "list[]" }{TEXT 413 1 
":" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 6 "op(L);" }}}{EXCHG 
{PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 6 "op(S);" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" 
{MPLTEXT 1 0 10 "seqL:=L[];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 
10 "seqS:=S[];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 22 "Coversely, to \+
convert " }{TEXT 294 8 "sequence" }{TEXT 413 4 " to " }{TEXT 295 3 "se
t" }{TEXT 413 4 " or " }{TEXT 296 4 "list" }{TEXT 413 11 ", just use "
 }{TEXT 297 11 "\{sequence\} " }{TEXT 413 3 "or " }{TEXT 298 10 "[sequ
ence]" }{TEXT 413 1 ":" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "> " 0 "" {MPLTEXT 1 0 28 "
\{seqL\},\{seqS\},[seqL],[seqS];" }}}{EXCHG {PARA 0 "" 0 "" {TEXT 413 
62 "We also can convert a positive integer between bases. convert(" }
